course: everyday
lesson: 360
title: Wasting food and water
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every day for three weeks, Noah in Austin threw his sandwich in the school bin because he did not like the crusts. Nobody noticed, and he thought he had found a clever trick. But he was hungry and grumpy every afternoon, and his maths got worse. Then a lunch helper saw the whole sandwich in the bin and told his teacher, who told Mum. At home there was a talk, not a row: Mum said «I make that every morning». Noah said sorry and asked for no crusts, and now he eats it all.
explain: Freddie left the tap running the whole time he brushed his teeth. Dad heard it and explained that a running tap can pour away several litres a minute — a bucket's worth in a couple of brushings. Freddie turns it off now while he brushes.
explain: Wasting is a quiet wrong: nobody cries at once. But food, water, paper and electricity cost money and someone's work, and some of them, like water, are scarce in many places.
explain: Getting away with wasting for weeks does not make it free; somebody paid for every sandwich in the bin.
fact: A running tap can pour away several litres of water every minute.
fact: Turning off the tap while brushing your teeth saves a lot of water every day.
fact: Food thrown away wastes the money, water, land and work that went into growing and making it.
fact: Lights left on in empty rooms waste electricity and make the bill higher.
fact: Paper is made from trees, so using both sides of a sheet saves trees.
fact: At a buffet, taking a little and going back for more wastes less than piling up a full plate.
fact: Spending all your money at once leaves nothing for things that come later.
fact: Losing or breaking things through carelessness means someone has to pay to replace them.
fact: Being hungry makes it harder to think and learn in the afternoon.
exercise: Why did Noah throw his sandwich away? | He did not like the crusts | He had already eaten his lunch | His teacher told him to do it | The sandwich had gone bad in his bag
exercise: Noah got away with it for three weeks. What was happening to him anyway? | He was hungry and his maths got worse | He was full and his maths improved | He got better at maths | Nothing changed at all
exercise: How was Noah found out? | A lunch helper saw the sandwich in the bin | He told everyone himself | His sandwich smelled in his bag and everyone noticed | His friend ate it
exercise: What put Noah's waste right? | Saying sorry and asking for no crusts | Hiding it in a different bin | Giving it to the birds every day | Asking for crisps instead
exercise: Why should you turn off the tap while brushing your teeth? | A running tap wastes several litres a minute | Running water makes the toothpaste stop working properly | The tap gets tired | It is quieter to brush without water
exercise: Kai spent all his birthday money on sweets in one day. What happened? | He had a tummy ache and no money left for later | He saved money by buying all his sweets in one big bag | The sweets lasted all month | Nothing, sweets are cheap
exercise: What would Kai do differently next time? | Save some of the money | Buy more sweets at once | Spend it all as fast as you can | Give it all away to friends
exercise: Grace left the lights on in every room. Why does this matter? | It wastes electricity and money | Lights need to stay on all day to keep working | It keeps burglars inside | Bulbs last longer when left on
exercise: Lily drew one line on each sheet and the paper pile was gone in a day. What was the wise choice? | Draw more on each sheet and use both sides | Ask the teacher for a much bigger pile of paper | Use only white paper | Draw smaller lines on new sheets
exercise: Ben threw away his apple after one bite. Mum saw it in the bin. What was the lesson? | Wasting food wastes money and work | Apples taste best with one bite | Bins are the right place for fruit you don't finish | Mum likes finding apples
exercise: Max broke his new toy by playing too rough. What did it cost him? | He could not play with it any more | He got a better toy | Nothing at all, toys fix themselves overnight | His friend's toy broke instead
exercise: Ruby left her coat at the park and lost it. What was a fair way to make up? | Saying sorry and paying a bit from her money | Saying coats always get lost at the park anyway | Asking for a more expensive one | Wearing her brother's coat forever
exercise: Leo piled his plate high at the buffet and left half. What would have been wiser? | Take a little and go back if still hungry | Pile it even higher so it lasts the whole party | Take only dessert | Eat it all even if he felt sick
exercise: Noah's friend says «I bin my lunch every day and my mum's never found out». What is true? | Being found out is not the point; food and money are wasted | If nobody ever finds out, then it is completely fine | Lunch helpers never check | Mums never make lunches again
exercise: Why does it matter that someone made Noah's sandwich? | Their work and money went into it | Sandwiches just make themselves somehow | It only matters for expensive food | It doesn't matter at all who made it
test: «hate crusts lol just bin it every day». What's the wiser move? | Ask for no crusts and eat the rest | Keep binning it, nobody will ever know | Hide it in your desk | Swap it for crisps every day
test: water's been running 2 minutes while u brush. Which is closest to how much is wasted? | Several litres | Just a few drops | One cup exactly | None, it goes back to the tap
test: binned my lunch all last month and no one noticed, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, the food was still wasted and you went hungry | Yes, if no one noticed last month then it's fine | Yes, bins are for lunch | Only on Fridays
test: Dad: «Who's left every light in the house on?!» Best reply if it was you? | «Me, sorry, I'll switch them off» | «The lights turn on by themselves» | «It's cheaper to leave them on all day» | «Not my lights, I didn't touch them»
test: «got £20 for my birthday, gonna spend it all on sweets today». What's likely? | A sore tummy and no money left for later | Sweets last much longer than money does, so it's fine | It's the best way to save | The shop gives change back later
test: «Miss, can I have more paper?» It's your 30th sheet, each with one line. What will she probably say? | Use the sheets you have, both sides | «Here's another 30 sheets, take as many as you like» | Paper is free, take the box | Go and buy some
test: buffet: kid comes back with a mountain of food and eats half. What's the smart way? | Take a little, go back for more if hungry | Take a mountain every time, just in case | Take one thing only | Don't eat at all
test: left my coat at the park, «mum'll just buy another». What's fairer? | Help pay for it and look after the next one | Ask for a dearer one | Say it was stolen | Blame your friend for not reminding you to take it
test: lunch helper sees you bin a full sandwich and says «that's a waste, love». What's the honest response? | «Sorry, I don't like the crusts» | «Not mine» | «I'm feeding the bin» | «Mind your own business, it's my sandwich»
test: teacher: «Why are you so tired every afternoon?» You've been skipping lunch. What's the honest answer? | «I've been throwing my lunch away» | «I don't know, Miss» | «Maths is just really boring in the afternoon» | «I'm always tired»
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the discarding of food is regarded as wasteful. | It squanders the money, resources and labour expended in its production | Food cannot be discarded legally | Food which has been discarded is replaced at no cost to anyone whatsoever | Food is of value only when expensive
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the leaving of a tap running whilst brushing the teeth? | It wastes a considerable volume of water each minute | It improves the efficacy of toothpaste | It wastes no water whatsoever, since all of it is recycled back into the tap | It is necessary for dental hygiene
exam: It is given that a pupil has discarded his lunch daily for three weeks without detection. Select the conclusion consistent with sound reasoning. | The absence of detection does not negate the waste or its effect on the pupil | The practice is justified by its success, since no harm has been detected | The waste ceases to exist if undetected | The pupil incurs no consequence whatever
exam: State the practice most consistent with the avoidance of waste at a self-service meal. | The taking of modest portions with return for more if required | The taking of the maximum available portion on the first visit to the table | The taking of dessert alone | The discarding of portions found unappealing
